sec.p:
Following Equation 35, we write relative fitnesses of the escape variant or transmissibility variant as
(2)
λE,W=ηβϕW(t), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \lambda_{E, W}=\eta \beta \phi_{W}(t), \tag{2} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(3)
λT,W=ρβS(t), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \lambda_{T, W}=\rho \beta S(t), \tag{3} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where β is the transmissibility coefficient, η is escape proportion against the wildtype, ρ is the variant’s proportional increase to transmissibility, and S(t) and ϕW are the proportions of the population who are susceptible and have wildtype immunity respectively.

We find that the mechanistic forms analyzed in this paper (Supplementary Text S1) can be represented as weighted combinations of B time-varying functions ϒb(t) with weights βb(v,u)\begin{document}$\beta_{b}^{(v, u)}$\end{document}. We can think of each of these functions ϒb as an immune background and the coefficient βb(v,u)\begin{document}$\beta_{b}^{(v, u)}$\end{document} as a transmission differential between variants v and u, so that
(5)
λv,u(t)=∑1≤b≤Bβb(v,u)Υb(t). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \lambda_{v, u}(t)=\sum_{1 \leq b \leq B} \beta_{b}^{(v, u)} \Upsilon_{b}(t) . \tag{5} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Even in the case of complete knowledge of the relative fitness and the underlying fitness contributions in the present and past, we have that change in the relative fitness is determined by
(6)
dλv,udt=∑1≤b≤Bβb(v,u)dΥbdt(t). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{d \lambda_{v, u}}{d t}=\sum_{1 \leq b \leq B} \beta_{b}^{(v, u)} \frac{d \Upsilon_{b}}{d t}(t) . \tag{6} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
By considering a Taylor expansion of the relative fitness about the point of estimation t0, we can approximate the relative fitness in the future as
(7)
λv,u(t)≈λv,u(t0)+(t−t0)∑1≤b≤Bβb(v,u)dΥbdt(t0). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \lambda_{v, u}(t) \approx \lambda_{v, u}\left(t_{0}\right)+\left(t-t_{0}\right) \sum_{1 \leq b \leq B} \beta_{b}^{(v, u)} \frac{d \Upsilon_{b}}{d t}\left(t_{0}\right) . \tag{7} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
This suggests small differences in the form of λv,u(t) can lead to meaningful differences in the future relative fitnesses through changes in the underlying immune backgrounds.

In this case, the overall growth rate r\begin{document}$r$\end{document} and relative incidence I(t)/I(0) can be written directly
(10)
r¯(t)=r¯(0)+[rW(t)−rW(0)]+Ψ(t), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \bar{r}(t)=\bar{r}(0)+\left[r_{W}(t)-r_{W}(0)\right]+\Psi(t), \tag{10} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(11)
I(t)I(0)=exp⁡(∫0t[rW(s)+Ψ(s)]ds) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{I(t)}{I(0)}=\exp \left(\int_{0}^{t}\left[r_{W}(s)+\Psi(s)\right] d s\right) \tag{11} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
using the cumulative selective pressure Ψ(t)=∫0tψ(s)ds\begin{document}$\Psi(t)=\int_{0}^{t} \psi(s) d s$\end{document}. In addition to estimating the relative fitness, metrics derived from these models can inform us of much more.

To generate smooth non-parametric estimates of variant growth rates, we develop a Gaussian process based model for relative fitnesses. That is, we model the relative fitness for each variant over time λv(t) as a multivariate normal distribution:
(14)
λv∼Normal(μ,Σ) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \boldsymbol{\lambda}_{v} \sim \operatorname{Normal}(\boldsymbol{\mu}, \boldsymbol{\Sigma}) \tag{14} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(15)
Σs,t=Kθ(s,t), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{s, t}=K_{\theta}(s, t), \tag{15} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where Kθ is a potentially parameterized kernel function. This induces a structure on the covariance of the relative fitness values over time points s and t, causing relative fitness to vary smoothly in time.

Now turning to the frequency dynamics of the population, we write the frequency of variant v in the population as fv(t)=Iv(t)/∑u=1VIu(t)\begin{document}$f_{v}(t)=I_{v}(t) / \sum_{u=1}^{V} I_{u}(t)$\end{document}. This allows us to derive an ODE for variant frequency in terms of the variant growth rates using the quotient rule for differentiation
(23)
dfvdt=fv(∑u=1V[rv(t)−ru(t)]fu) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{d f_{v}}{d t}=f_{v}\left(\sum_{u=1}^{V}\left[r_{v}(t)-r_{u}(t)\right] f_{u}\right) \tag{23} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(24)
=fv(rv(t)−∑u=1Vru(t)fu). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} =f_{v}\left(r_{v}(t)-\sum_{u=1}^{V} r_{u}(t) f_{u}\right) . \tag{24} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
This system of differential equations resembles a logistic growth equation and can be shown to have the following solution in terms of the initial frequencies fv(0) and the variant growth rates
(25)
fv(t)=fv(0)exp⁡(∫0trv(s)ds)∑u=1Vfu(0)exp⁡(∫0tru(s)ds). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} f_{v}(t)=\frac{f_{v}(0) \exp \left(\int_{0}^{t} r_{v}(s) d s\right)}{\sum_{u=1}^{V} f_{u}(0) \exp \left(\int_{0}^{t} r_{u}(s) d s\right)} . \tag{25} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
The above representation of the variant frequency will serve as a centerpiece for many of the arguments to follow. We see that by tracking the rate at which variant viruses are spreading, we can construct the corresponding frequency dynamics without knowing the absolute prevalence of any variant.

Using the above equation for the variant frequencies, we can write the relative frequency of variant v over u as xv,u(t) = fv(t)/fu(t) to see
(26)
xv,u(t)=fv(t)fu(t)=fv(0)fu(0)exp⁡(∫0t[rv(s)−ru(s)]ds) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} x_{v, u}(t)=\frac{f_{v}(t)}{f_{u}(t)}=\frac{f_{v}(0)}{f_{u}(0)} \exp \left(\int_{0}^{t}\left[r_{v}(s)-r_{u}(s)\right] d s\right) \tag{26} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(27)
=xv,u(0)exp⁡(∫0tλv,u(s)ds). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} =x_{v, u}(0) \exp \left(\int_{0}^{t} \lambda_{v, u}(s) d s\right) . \tag{27} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Notice this relative frequency change depends on the initial relative frequencies and the relative fitness λv,u(t) = rv(t) − ru(t) of v over u. This relative fitness has the same units as the exponential growth rate (e.g. per day). Using the definition of relative fitness, we can notice that
(28)
λv,u(t)=rv(t)−ru(t)=ddt[log⁡(xv,u(t))]=−λu,v(t). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \lambda_{v, u}(t)=r_{v}(t)-r_{u}(t)=\frac{d}{d t}\left[\log \left(x_{v, u}(t)\right)\right]=-\lambda_{u, v}(t) . \tag{28} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
We can see that there is a symmetry in the relative fitnesses and that the associated frequency dynamics depend on the differences between relative fitnesses. This suggests that absolute fitness (in terms of the growth of infections) may not be inferable from frequencies alone. This definition of relative fitness becomes essential in describing various existing modeling approaches for frequency dynamic data and motivates possible extensions since we can represent these models as having the form:
(29)
fv(t)=fv(0)exp⁡(∫0tλv,v∗(s)ds)∑u=1Vfu(0)exp⁡(∫0tλu,v∗(s)ds), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} f_{v}(t)=\frac{f_{v}(0) \exp \left(\int_{0}^{t} \lambda_{v, v^{*}}(s) d s\right)}{\sum_{u=1}^{V} f_{u}(0) \exp \left(\int_{0}^{t} \lambda_{u, v^{*}}(s) d s\right)}, \tag{29} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where the relative fitness of v is expressed as relative to an arbitrary pivot variant v∗.

For simplicity, we will begin by analyzing a two-strain SIR model in which a variant virus v can differ from wildtype virus wt by increased intrinsic transmissibility (via ηT) and immune escape against wild-type immunity (via ηE). This gives a system of 5 ordinary differential equations
(30)
dSdt=−βSIW−β(1+ρ)SIv, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{d S}{d t}=-\beta S I_{W}-\beta(1+\rho) S I_{v}, \tag{30} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(31)
dIWdt=βSIW−γIW, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{d I_{W}}{d t}=\beta S I_{W}-\gamma I_{W}, \tag{31} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(32)
dIvdt=β(1+ρ)SIv+β(1+ρ)ηϕWIv−γIv, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{d I_{v}}{d t}=\beta(1+\rho) S I_{v}+\beta(1+\rho) \eta \phi_{W} I_{v}-\gamma I_{v}, \tag{32} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(33)
dϕWdt=γIW−β(1+ρ)ηϕWIv, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{d \phi_{W}}{d t}=\gamma I_{W}-\beta(1+\rho) \eta \phi_{W} I_{v}, \tag{33} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(34)
dϕvdt=γIv, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{d \phi_{v}}{d t}=\gamma I_{v}, \tag{34} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where IW denotes wild-type prevalence, Iv denotes variant prevalence and ϕW denotes immunity derived from wild-type infection. In this model, the variant virus can infect both susceptible individuals S and individuals with immunity to wild-type virus ϕW . Increased intrinsic transmissibility increases the baseline transmission rate from β in wild-type to β(1 + ρ) in the variant virus and immune escape increases the transmission rate against those with wildtype immunity, so that the at-risk population is ηϕW .

This model can also be extended to an n-strain SIR model where each variant strain vi with 2 ≤ i ≤ n is described by its own advantage parameters θi = (ρ(i), η(i)) relative to the wildtype (θW = θ1 = (0, 0))
(36)
dSdt=−βSIW−∑iβ(1+ρi)SIvi \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{d S}{d t}=-\beta S I_{W}-\sum_{i} \beta\left(1+\rho_{i}\right) S I_{v_{i}} \tag{36} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(37)
dIWdt=βSIW−γIW \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{d I_{W}}{d t}=\beta S I_{W}-\gamma I_{W} \tag{37} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(38)
dIvidt=β(1+ρi)SIvi+β(1+ρi)ηiϕWIvi−γIvi \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{d I_{v_{i}}}{d t}=\beta\left(1+\rho_{i}\right) S I_{v_{i}}+\beta\left(1+\rho_{i}\right) \eta_{i} \phi_{W} I_{v_{i}}-\gamma I_{v_{i}} \tag{38} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(39)
dϕWdt=γIW−∑iβ(1+ρi)ηiϕWIvi \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{d \phi_{W}}{d t}=\gamma I_{W}-\sum_{i} \beta\left(1+\rho_{i}\right) \eta_{i} \phi_{W} I_{v_{i}} \tag{39} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(40)
dϕvidt=γIvi,i∈{2,…,n}. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{d \phi_{v_{i}}}{d t}=\gamma I_{v_{i}}, \quad i \in\{2, \ldots, n\} . \tag{40} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
In this formulation, the variant viruses compete only for susceptible population and those with previous wild-type infection. This formulation can be generalized to allow for competition between all variants for any exposure history and will be discussed in the following sections. In Fig. S1, we implement and simulate a 3-strain model with wildtype as above, an escape variant E with θ2 = (0, η), and a transmissibility increase variant T with θ3 = (ρ, 0).

We can then write the system of ordinary differential equations as
(41)
dIvdt=β∑1≤b≤Bηv,bϕbIv−γIv,v=1,…,V \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{d I_{v}}{d t}=\beta \sum_{1 \leq b \leq B} \eta_{v, b} \phi_{b} I_{v}-\gamma I_{v}, \quad v=1, \ldots, V \tag{41} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(42)
dϕbdt=−β∑1≤v≤Vηv,bϕbIv+∑v:bv=bγIv. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{d \phi_{b}}{d t}=-\beta \sum_{1 \leq v \leq V} \eta_{v, b} \phi_{b} I_{v}+\sum_{v: b_{v}=b} \gamma I_{v} . \tag{42} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
With this model, susceptible and recovered compartments in the standard SIR model can be thought of as immune backgrounds. This allows us to represent the standard SIR model as S = ϕS, I = IW , R = ϕW and ηW,S = 1, ηW,W = 0 and bW = W . We can also think of the two-strain SIR with ρ = 1 as a special case of this model where we set S = ϕS, ηW,S = 1, ηW,W = 0, ηv,S = 1, ηv,W = η and keep all other parameters the same.

Here, infection can occur from any individual infected with strain i assuming their past immune history does not include i and the infected are any recovered individual with immune history J RJ . To compute the strain growth rate, we can sum over all possible immune histories for individuals infected with strain i, so that
(48)
dIidt=∑J∈P(M),i∉JdRJIidt \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{d I_{i}}{d t}=\sum_{J \in P(M), i \notin J} \frac{d R_{J} I_{i}}{d t} \tag{48} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(49)
=−γiIi+∑J∈P(M),i∉Jβi,JRJ∑K∈P(M),i∉KRKIi \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} =-\gamma_{i} I_{i}+\sum_{J \in P(M), i \notin J} \beta_{i, J} R_{J} \sum_{K \in P(M), i \notin K} R_{K} I_{i} \tag{49} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(50)
=−γiIi+∑J∈P(M),i∉Jβi,JRJIi \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} =-\gamma_{i} I_{i}+\sum_{J \in P(M), i \notin J} \beta_{i, J} R_{J} I_{i} \tag{50} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(51)
=(−γi+∑J∈P(M),i∉Jβi,JRJ)Ii \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} =\left(-\gamma_{i}+\sum_{J \in P(M), i \notin J} \beta_{i, J} R_{J}\right) I_{i} \tag{51} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(52)
=(−γ+β∑J∈P(M),i∉Jηi,JRJ)Ii. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} =\left(-\gamma+\beta \sum_{J \in P(M), i \notin J} \eta_{i, J} R_{J}\right) I_{i} . \tag{52} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(53)
Assuming that the transmission rate can be decomposed as a base transmission rate β and a strain i and immune history J specific escape rate ηi,J and that the recovery rate is constant, we notice this is identical to our previous immune background model. Therefore, our relative fitnesses simplify to
(54)
λi,j=β∑B∈P(M)(ηi,B−ηj,B)RB, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \lambda_{i, j}=\beta \sum_{B \in P(M)}\left(\eta_{i, B}-\eta_{j, B}\right) R_{B}, \tag{54} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where for simplicity we define ηv,B = 0 if v ∈ B.

Beginning again from our assumption of inhomogeneous exponential growth, we can write a differential equation for the total prevalence I(t) = ∑v Iv(t),
(55)
dIdt=∑vdIvdt=∑vrv(t)Iv(t) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{d I}{d t}=\sum_{v} \frac{d I_{v}}{d t}=\sum_{v} r_{v}(t) I_{v}(t) \tag{55} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(56)
=(∑vrv(t)fv(t))I(t), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} =\left(\sum_{v} r_{v}(t) f_{v}(t)\right) I(t), \tag{56} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where we’ve used that Iv(t) = fv(t)I(t). This allows us to see that r¯(t)=∑vrv(t)fv(t)\begin{document}$\bar{r}(t)=\sum_{v} r_{v}(t) f_{v}(t)$\end{document} is the average growth rate of the prevalence. Re-writing the average in terms of some base exponential growth rate and the relative fitnesses so that rv(t) = λv(t) + rW (t), we get that r¯(t)=∑vλv(t)fv(t)+rW(t)\begin{document}$\bar{r}(t)=\sum_{v} \lambda_{v}(t) f_{v}(t)+r_{W}(t)$\end{document}. We can simplify this by writing r¯(t)=λ¯(t)+rW(t)\begin{document}$\bar{r}(t)=\bar{\lambda}(t)+r_{W}(t)$\end{document} where λ(t)=∑vλv(t)fv(t)\begin{document}$\lambda(t)=\sum_{v} \lambda_{v}(t) f_{v}(t)$\end{document} is the mean fitness of the population. We can now look at the rate of change in the average growth rate by taking its derivative
(57)
dr¯dt=drWdt+dλdt \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{d \bar{r}}{d t}=\frac{d r_{W}}{d t}+\frac{d \lambda}{d t} \tag{57} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(58)
=drWdt+∑v[dλvdtfv(t)+λv(t)dfvdt] \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} =\frac{d r_{W}}{d t}+\sum_{v}\left[\frac{d \lambda_{v}}{d t} f_{v}(t)+\lambda_{v}(t) \frac{d f_{v}}{d t}\right] \tag{58} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(59)
=drWdt+∑v[dλvdtfv+λvfv(λv−λ¯)] \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} =\frac{d r_{W}}{d t}+\sum_{v}\left[\frac{d \lambda_{v}}{d t} f_{v}+\lambda_{v} f_{v}\left(\lambda_{v}-\bar{\lambda}\right)\right] \tag{59} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(60)
=drWdt+∑vdλvdtfv(t)+∑vλv(λv−λ¯)fv \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} =\frac{d r_{W}}{d t}+\sum_{v} \frac{d \lambda_{v}}{d t} f_{v}(t)+\sum_{v} \lambda_{v}\left(\lambda_{v}-\bar{\lambda}\right) f_{v} \tag{60} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(61)
=drWdt+∑vdλvdtfv(t)+∑vλv2fv−λ¯∑vλvfv \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} =\frac{d r_{W}}{d t}+\sum_{v} \frac{d \lambda_{v}}{d t} f_{v}(t)+\sum_{v} \lambda_{v}^{2} f_{v}-\bar{\lambda} \sum_{v} \lambda_{v} f_{v} \tag{61} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(62)
=drWdt+Ef(t)[dλvdt]+Vf(t)[λv]. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} =\frac{d r_{W}}{d t}+\mathbb{E}_{f(t)}\left[\frac{d \lambda_{v}}{d t}\right]+\mathbb{V}_{f(t)}\left[\lambda_{v}\right] . \tag{62} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Here, we’ve written the last line in terms of expectations relative to sampling according to the frequency distribution. This shows us that the change in the average growth rate of the epidemic can be written in terms of the growth rate of the pivot category, the mean rate of change in the relative fitness, and the variance of the relative fitnesses. We will call terms which can be computed in terms of quantities derived from frequencies alone the selective pressure
(63)
ψ(t)=Ef(t)[dλvdt]+Vf(t)[λv]. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \psi(t)=\mathbb{E}_{f(t)}\left[\frac{d \lambda_{v}}{d t}\right]+\mathbb{V}_{f(t)}\left[\lambda_{v}\right] . \tag{63} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
We can use this idea to directly write the prevalence in terms of the selective pressure and the base growth rate. First, we define a cumulative selective pressure
(64)
Ψ(t)=∫0tψ(s)ds. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \Psi(t)=\int_{0}^{t} \psi(s) d s . \tag{64} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
We can then use this to reconstruct the relative incidence
(65)
I(t)I(0)=exp⁡(∫0tr¯(s)ds) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{I(t)}{I(0)}=\exp \left(\int_{0}^{t} \bar{r}(s) d s\right) \tag{65} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(66)
=exp⁡(∫0t[rW(s)+Ψ(s)]ds). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} =\exp \left(\int_{0}^{t}\left[r_{W}(s)+\Psi(s)\right] d s\right) . \tag{66} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
